<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">

  <!--
    Windows application manifest. It exists for ONE line: dpiAwareness.

    WebView2 requires PerMonitorV2. Without it the process ends up
    PER_MONITOR_AWARE (v1) — tao calls the older `SetProcessDpiAwareness`, which
    cannot express V2 — and WebView2 then treats the window's PHYSICAL client size
    as its CSS viewport while still rasterising at devicePixelRatio. On any
    display above 100% the page is laid out dpr times too large for its window.
    Measured on a 125% display:

        native client   1280 x 800 device px
        page laid out   1280 x 800 CSS px  ->  1600 x 1000 device px
        cut off         320 px right, 200 px bottom

    Every screen silently lost its right and bottom fifth. In the Review player
    that is precisely where the Timeline / Bookmark / Export buttons and the whole
    scrubbable timeline live, so they rendered off-window and were unreachable.
    The timeline was never broken — it could not be seen.

    Why a manifest and not a runtime call: a process's DPI awareness is locked by
    whoever sets it first, and tao sets v1 during its own startup. Calling
    `SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext` from `main` loses that race (verified: the
    import is in the binary, the window still came up v1). A manifest is applied
    at process creation, before any code runs, so tao's call simply fails.

    Why ONLY dpiAwareness, with no <dpiAware> sibling: with both present Windows
    took `true/pm` — plain per-monitor v1 — and ignored PerMonitorV2, leaving the
    exact bug this file is here to fix. The 2005 element is for pre-Windows-10
    hosts, and this app needs WebView2, which is Windows 10+. Do not add it back.

    Verify after touching this file, do not assume:
      * GetAwarenessFromDpiAwarenessContext on the main window -> PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2
      * in the page, innerWidth * devicePixelRatio == native client width
      * devicePixelRatio stays > 1 (dpr 1 means Windows is bitmap-upscaling and
        the whole UI has gone soft — that is a different failure, not a fix)
  -->

  <!-- Visual styles. Tauri's default manifest carries this; replacing the
       manifest means carrying it too, or common controls render unthemed. -->
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"
      />
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>

  <!-- PerMonitorV2 is only honoured when the app declares Windows 10 support. -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/> <!-- 10 / 11 -->
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

</assembly>
